Chapter 28 – Part 2
Curtis sealed the last bag.
He turned, kit already under his arm.
“I got my kit but I don’t got time to set up. There’s a shitty motel nearby we can work in and check. I’ll ping you my location—”
A click at the door.
Key. Cheap metal. Home.
Curtis did not finish the sentence.
He went over the balcony rail like the height was a rumor. White mist kissed the concrete on the way down and ate the landing. No crack this time. No student on a ledge. No umbrella coat in the window when the door swung.
The room he left was only Celty’s life again: hamper, couch, office, a dullahan standing in her own entry with someone else’s late text in her pocket.
Shinra’s voice arrived first, bright, carrying bags.
“Celty? You’re home? I thought you were on a job. I told you I was running late—Mei wanted coffee.”
Mei’s laugh followed him in.
Celty’s smoke held still. The private channel snapped after the falling boy.
You jumped.
Then, colder, useful:
Go. I will be the person who lives here. Send the pin. If they look at the balcony I will say a bird.
Curtis was already a black coat in an alley three floors down, breath still unfairly even, bags against his ribs.
He thumbed the pin to her while he walked, not ran — running after a balcony was how people got remembered.
motel. 8 min. room under a fake name. don’t come if they follow you.
On a Kishitani balcony that had not broken,
the detective left no student behind.
Shinra Kishitani came home late with coffee and Mei.
Celty Sturluson stood in the doorway as if she had never pointed at a hamper.
Curtis Nightstar hit the street with underwear in plastic
and a motel waiting to tell the truth.
Celty lifted the PDA before Shinra could finish unpacking the coffee story.
Stopping in.
Helmet strap broke on a run.
Grabbing the spare and going back out.
Shinra’s face did the concern he always did when she stood in a doorway.
“You could sit for five minutes. Mei bought the good beans. Late or not, you look like you haven’t been home in a day.”
Mei watched the hallway, the office door, the balcony light. Then she smiled like a colleague.
“Spare helmet. Very complete. Don’t let us keep a working woman.”
Celty typed once more.
I’ll text when I’m done.
She took the spare from the hook she always took it from. She did not look at the hamper. She did not look at the desk. She did not look at the rail a foreigner in an umbrella coat had used as a staircase.
Shinra bought it because he wanted her safe and moving.
Mei bought it because a courier who did not sit was easier than a courier who started opening closets.
The door shut on coffee and someone else’s late.
On the stairs, the private channel opened toward a pin on a map.
They bought it. Both of them. I am leaving. If the room is under a fake name, make it one I can say at a desk without sounding like a kidnapper.
A thinner beat.
And next time you jump off my balcony, warn me before the key turns. I had to become a person who lives here in less than a second.
Shooter waited in the street like it had never been anything else.
Celty Sturluson became a stop-in
and a spare helmet.
Shinra Kishitani believed the strap.
Mei believed the exit.
The sample was already across the ward in a shitty motel
that did not care what anyone bought.
The motel looked like it charged by the hour and washed the sheets by rumor.
Celty knocked once. The door opened on a black cap, a mask, an umbrella on a stolen coat, and a desk that had become a bench.
Curtis did not look up from a slide.
“Sorry for jumping without warning. It would be harder to explain if I was there.”
Bags sat in a line. Labels in his handwriting. Mei. Couch. Chair. Hamper. The cheap lamp made everything look like evidence.
Celty shut the door with her heel. Helmet off. Smoke low. The private channel came through first, still scraped from the key in her own lock.
Harder to explain a man in my living room. Easier to explain a bird. Fine.
Then, out loud in the way only he would hear:
“They bought the helmet strap. Shinra wanted me to sit. Mei wanted me to leave. I left.” She stepped closer to the desk, not to the bed. “Do not apologize for the jump. Apologize if you mixed the bags.”
Curtis’s eyes creased.
“Didn’t mix them. I’m not that kind of detective.” He held a swab to the light, then set it down like it had offended him. “Give me a few minutes. This kit was meant for a kitchen table and a night we already missed. Motel water is a crime of its own.”
Celty stood at his shoulder. Yellow helmet under her arm. Empty space above her collar aimed at the plastic.
“Plain language when you have it. Not later. Not kind.”
“I remember.”
The lamp hummed. Somewhere a couple argued through the wall. Ikebukuro went on being a city that did not know a dullahan and a boy in a convention coat were about to name a stain.
On a rented desk that would never be clean,
the sample finally sat still.
Celty Sturluson had become a spare helmet
and a closed door.
Curtis Nightstar worked in stolen science and let the apology
be the last soft thing in the room.
Celty did not sit.
The vent arrived while he was still matching a swab to a card, like the motel lamp had given her permission the apartment never would.
“Yesterday. I was with Shizuo.” The private channel stayed even at first. Work-voice. Then it frayed. “A girl. Scar on her neck. We chased. We lost them.”
Curtis’s hand did not stop. The mask hid the rest.
“Seiji Yagiri was with her. He stabbed Shizuo with a pen. A pen. Shizuo still dropped him.” Smoke ticked once above her collar. “I had the street and I still lost a girl who should not have been able to vanish that clean. Shizuo was bleeding and laughing and angry in the same breath. I went home to a clean desk. I came here to plastic bags. I am tired of chasing things that disappear the moment I blink.”
She stood over the kit like the slides might confess a neck.
“Yagiri Pharmaceuticals. That name is on too many doors. Shinra laughs when I say it. Mei laughs like she already knows the joke. A boy with a pen thinks he can put Shizuo Heiwajima on the ground. And I still do not have my head.”
Curtis labeled a bag. The umbrella on his coat felt suddenly loud.
“Shizuo’s alive?”
“Of course he is. It was a pen.” A thinner beat. “That is not the point. The point is I had her and then I did not. The point is I am in a motel watching you scrape underwear because my house lied, and yesterday I could not even keep a scarred girl in my line of sight.”
He did not say Mikado. He did not say tea. He did not say I found her.
He said, careful, through the mask:
“A scar on the neck and a Yagiri boy. That’s not a random couple on a date.”
“No.” Celty’s smoke flattened. “It is a product walking. I felt it. I still lost them.”
Curtis set the swab down before his fingers could tell on him.
“We’ll finish this first. Then you can tell me the street. One crime scene at a time or we’ll start inventing answers.”
The lamp hummed. The girl with the scar sat in a first-year’s room across the ward, drinking bad tea, while a dullahan vented about losing her.
On a motel desk, two hunts occupied the same air.
Celty Sturluson had lost a neck
and a day.
Curtis Nightstar listened like a detective and kept a kettle
off the record.
Celty’s smoke thinned.
“I—I missed you.”
It did not belong in a motel that charged by the hour. It arrived anyway, after a pen, a scar, a clean desk, and a balcony.
Curtis looked up.
He pulled the mask down. Cap still on. Mismatched eyes on the empty space above her collar, not on the slides.
“I missed you too, Celty.” Plain. No performance. “But we gotta work so I can get your answer.”
He left the mask under his chin. The missed you stayed in the room like a second lamp. He turned back to the card because she had asked for plain language, not a soft landing in the middle of someone else’s underwear.
Celty stood there a second longer.
I know. The link was quieter than the vent. I know. I only needed to say it before the answer made the room colder.
Curtis nodded once, already reaching for the next swab.
“Then stay. Watch. If I’m wrong, you can be mad at the kit. If I’m right…” He did not finish. She already knew what right would cost a kitchen across the ward.
The mask stayed down. The work did not.
On a rented desk, missing each other
got one sentence and then a label.
Celty Sturluson said it first.
Curtis Nightstar said it back and kept his hands
on the truth she had asked him not to make kind.
Curtis pulled two more bags from the kit.
Locks of hair. Paper cups, flattened and labeled.
Celty’s smoke snapped.
“Wait. Why did you take those?”
“When I was looking through Shinra’s things.” He did not dress it up. “He’s very clean. So if DNA shows up, I need these for cross-examination and reference.” A small tilt of the cap. “Couch stain without a control is just a sad story. Hair from his brush. Cup from the sink. That’s how you stop guessing.”
He opened his phone, grimaced at the screen.
“I was in such a rush I forgot my laptop. My phone will have to suffice.”
The motel lamp made the cups look stolen. They were. From a house she lived in. From a man who had texted that he was running late.
Celty stood over the bags like they might bite.
You took his hair.
“I took a reference. I didn’t take a trophy.” Curtis thumbed an app that did not belong in a first-year’s camera roll. “If the underwear is only Mei, that’s one sentence. If it’s Mei and him, that’s another. If it’s a third person, we have a different problem. I can’t say any of that without something that is definitely his.”
A beat.
“I’ll burn the extras when we’re done. I’m not starting a shrine.”
Celty’s gloves flexed.
“…Fine. Reference.” The link stayed tight. “Do not tell me later that you kept a cup because it was useful. Useful becomes a habit.”
“Copy.”
He photographed labels. Logged times. The phone’s glow washed the umbrella logo red-and-white and wrong.
On a motel desk, a clean man became a control sample.
Celty Sturluson watched her home turn into bags.
Curtis Nightstar forgot a laptop and made a phone
do detective work anyway.
An hour went by like motel time always did — thin walls, bad lamp, a city that did not know it was being used as a lab.
Curtis reviewed school notes between cycles because Raira still existed. Class reps. A desk he had fallen out of. A lie about a hotel. The umbrella coat sat on the chair like a third person.
The phone dinged.
Processed.
He opened the results.
His brow furrowed.
Not surprise. Confirmation had a face, and it was this one: the couch, the chair, the desk grain, Mei’s underwear, Shinra’s hamper, the hair, the cup. Two profiles. Overlap where overlap should not have been if the kitchen laugh had been only coffee.
No third ghost. No stranger. The house had not been broken into.
It had been used.
Celty saw the furrow before he spoke.
“Curtis.”
He did not look up yet. The mask was still under his chin. The notes were still open under his elbow like a boy who had somewhere else to be in the morning.
“Yeah.” A short breath. “Give me a second to say it the way you asked.”
Plain. Not kind.
The phone glowed between them with a sentence that would follow her back to a kettle.
On a rented desk, school notes
and a house
finished processing at the same time.
Celty Sturluson watched his brow
and already knew
the kind of answer
that made a room colder.
Curtis Nightstar stared at two names that were not a maybe
and got ready to stop being gentle.
Curtis turned the phone.
No speech first. The screen.
“Couch sample. Two profiles. They clash because they aren’t separate stains sitting next to each other. They’re mixed. Body fluids from intercourse.” He flicked to the next log. “Mei’s underwear. Same profile as the woman on the couch. Shinra’s cup. Shinra’s hair. Same profile as the man on the couch.” Another tap. Clinical. Ugly. “The fluid came back as sexual fluids. Not coffee. Not cream. Not a spill they were embarrassed about.”
He set the phone down between them so she would not have to lean into his hand.
“That’s the sentence. They had sex in your house. On the furniture you live with. Then they cleaned it when they came home.”
The motel lamp hummed like it wanted out.
Celty did not move.
Black smoke rose in a thin, wrong ribbon from the empty space above her collar. The private channel arrived late, as if it had to climb out of a desk that had already been wiped once.
…Both of them.
Not a question.
Then, smaller:
“Say the last part again.”
“Sexual fluids. Mixed. His reference matches. Hers matches. The couch is not a maybe.”
Celty’s gloves closed until the leather complained.
She had asked for plain language. She had asked him not to be kind. The room obeyed.
On a shitty motel desk, a house
got its name.
Celty Sturluson looked at two people
who had laughed in her kitchen
over good beans.
Curtis Nightstar held the phone like a verdict
and did not look away while she read it.
Celty?”
The name sat on the cheap wood like a hand offered, not a push.
Celty’s smoke did not settle.
“That’s why… that’s why it didn’t feel wrong to have sex with you.”
Not an excuse aimed at him. A fact landing in the wreckage of a couch sample. The kitchen laugh. The clean desk. The night she had asked for his arms and then more, and morning had not invented regret.
If the house was already a lie, the motel of two nights ago stopped being the only betrayal in the story.
Curtis went still.
Mask under his chin. Cap on. Phone dark between them. He did not reach. He did not smile. He did not say I told you at sushi.
“…Celty.” Softer. “Look at me a second.”
He waited until the empty space above her collar was on him, not the screen.
“What they did on your furniture is theirs. What happened with us is not a receipt you have to justify because their DNA mixed.” A short breath. “You don’t owe this result a reason you were allowed to want someone.”
His voice stayed even. The furrow stayed.
“If you’re saying the lie made room — I hear you. If you’re saying we only exist because they did — I’m going to disagree, but not tonight like a debate club.” He nodded once at the bags. “Tonight you get to be angry. You don’t have to turn it into a thesis about my bed.”
The wall next door laughed at something stupid.
Celty’s gloves were still fists.
On a rented desk, a verdict
tried to rewrite a night.
Celty Sturluson found a why
in mixed DNA.
Curtis Nightstar would not let a couch stain
be the only name
for what she had asked for
when she missed him.